// --- The `piece` curation pass (2026-09-02, owner-ruled; plan 0022) ---------
// `piece` means "a whole one of these, and we have nothing better to call it".
// Where the vocabulary DOES have something better — a clove, an avocado, a
// medium potato — `piece` is not offered at all, so nobody (human or machine)
// ever has to guess which one a `piece` meant. That is an ADMISSION fact in
// the explicit `allowed_units` list (ADR-0008 §4, ADR-0010): no new column, no
// runtime cascade, no size-word guessing.
//
// The ruling is NOT rule-based and is not derived: every one of the 142 seeded
// ingredients that carries a measure was read by hand and ruled on by the
// owner (docs/exec-plans/active/0022-piece-curation.md, "Owner rulings —
// 2026-09-02"). Sections A and B were accepted as drafted; C and D were ruled
// row by row; the analogies are marked as such. Net: `piece` stays on NO
// seeded ingredient that carries a measure. The ~149 measure-less rows are
// untouched — they keep `piece`, because there is nothing clearer to say, and
// `default_allowed_units()` (and its Dart mirror) is deliberately NOT changed.
//
// 66 of the 142 removals are already no-ops today: those rows are mass- or
// volume-default, and `default_allowed_units()` never admits `piece` for them.
// They are written anyway — the file is the decision record, and the ruling
// must survive a vocab row later flipping its default unit to `piece`.
//
// Three measure edits the owner ruled in the same pass ride along below.
{"kind": "drop_measure", "match_text": "broccoli", "label": "bunch", "reason": "owner 2026-09-02 (\"bunch -> piece\"): the 608 g bunch is what a recipe means by \"1 broccoli\" — relabelled `whole` below so the whole reads as one, which is what lets `piece` come out of the list"}
{"kind": "add_measure", "match_text": "broccoli", "label": "whole", "basis_amount": 608, "sort_order": 0, "source": "usda_fdc:170379 (1 bunch)", "reason": "owner 2026-09-02: the same USDA 1-bunch portion under the name a recipe uses — a whole broccoli. Provenance kept verbatim from the row it replaces; only the label changed"}
{"kind": "drop_measure", "match_text": "cherry tomato", "label": "cherry", "reason": "owner 2026-09-02 (\"cherry is wrong... drop piece / cherry / tomato altogether\"): cherry tomatoes are measured by the cup or by weight, and the borrowed generic-tomato `1 cherry` portion only invited a count nobody writes"}
{"kind": "add_measure", "match_text": "ginger", "label": "piece, 1 inch", "basis_amount": 12, "sort_order": 1, "source": "seed:typical", "reason": "owner 2026-09-02 asked for a countable piece of ginger; recipes count a 1-inch / thumb-sized piece. USDA's 2.2 g slice is a thin 1\"-diameter round, NOT a 1-inch piece, so this carries its own typical weight (10-15 g band) and AWAITS THE OWNER'S CONFIRMATION — tracked in docs/exec-plans/tech-debt-tracker.md. `slice` stays"}
